v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}40 & -2 \\\\ 35 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -56 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534615 17592186045417 true] [17592186427864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427862 13194139534615 17592186045417 true] [17592186427864 1069 "q-37605" 13194139534615 17592186045417 true] [17592186427864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534615 17592186045417 true] [17592186427864 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534615 17592186045417 true] [17592186427864 1072 #uuid "44ed4b4e-a305-51c9-9d7d-957ba67e3cad" 13194139534615 17592186045417 true] [17592186427864 1074 "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139534615 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-26a52f5c-fb5a-567f-a05c-b4a3377da12a" 17592186427851 "answer-271153a7-e008-5233-9e22-3f12deb489c2" 17592186427852 "answer-349ea5d5-1623-5b94-b22b-a5a6bd4fb201" 17592186427853 "answer-57dd64ab-9583-5552-b2ec-3be32f9e1309" 17592186427854 "answer-5e3acd5e-d728-5511-ba7b-b5202ac2ddfb" 17592186427855 "answer-63ef4572-30b7-56ef-8f5a-ac03a89540d1" 17592186427856 "answer-65b8a476-fed9-5e7d-acb6-3aec390dd318" 17592186427857 "answer-7154f91d-3460-5bf3-bea9-dc1e61a7a77f" 17592186427858 "answer-b20dbe0a-b3d3-556e-baa9-349554260ed9" 17592186427859 "answer-f3482122-1b23-51b6-bab9-0274990d6acf" 17592186427860 "answer-field-6a4b5644-3578-5ae2-81ef-1e895ed96291" 17592186427861 "answer-field-ef6614e3-dc36-51f8-9c16-862fd38745d1" 17592186427862 "question-403fa166-7921-5239-b399-2bac8767c7a0" 17592186427863 "question-44ed4b4e-a305-51c9-9d7d-957ba67e3cad" 17592186427864} :edb/committ